Package: mos-board-virt-arm64
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: mos-system (= @VERSION@), mos-kernel-virt-arm64 (= @VERSION@), ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: mos board support -- virt-arm64 (QEMU aarch64 virt, UEFI, A/B layout)
 Everything the image carries because it is the virt-arm64 board: /etc/fstab
 and the ESP mount unit rendered from boards/virt-arm64/board.env, the RAUC
 system configuration for its grub backend, and /usr/bin/grub-editenv. The
 ESP's own grub.cfg is not in here: the image assembler renders it from the
 repository, so a device copy would be an unrendered template nothing reads.
 .
 The board is QEMU's aarch64 `virt` machine booted through AAVMF, the same
 architecture the shipping device runs and the same UEFI + GRUB boot flow x64
 uses. It exists so that arm64 claims can be made by RUNNING arm64 code:
 cx3576 is arm64 and is real hardware, so before this board every arm64
 assertion in the tree was either static or owed to a bench.
 .
 There is no vendor boot chain behind this board -- the firmware provides it --
 but the KERNEL is this repository's, built by boards/virt-arm64/bsp and
 packaged as mos-kernel-virt-arm64, which this package depends on. That is
 forced rather than preferred: the boot contract is a dm-mod.create= verity
 table on the kernel command line with no initrd, which needs CONFIG_DM_INIT,
 and Debian's linux-image-arm64 does not set it.
 .
 grub-editenv's libdevmapper is not named here: it is an ELF dependency, so
 ${shlibs:Depends} derives it, and with the version dpkg-shlibdeps reads.
